iable is a sentence that contains the variable defined on some *universal set* and has the property that when we substitute any concrete value of the variable into the predicate, the result becomes a statement that is either true or false.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our options. \n\n- Option I is a predicate. Substituting a concrete value for $n$ into the sentence, we get a statement that is either true or false.\n\n- Option II is a predicate. Substituting a concrete value for $x$ into the sentence, we get a statement that is either true or false.\n\n- Option III is a predicate. Substituting a concrete value for $y$ into the sentence, we get a statement that is either true or false.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I, II, and III.\""] ["question-f4fa3c45-5e91-57f0-a165-1adda22fdfd5" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy] ["answer-field-dd1c7fcd-33d6-50f3-a903-c3e604c9e00d" :answer-field/id #uuid "dd1c7fcd-33d6-50f3-a903-c3e604c9e00d"] ["answer-ce19c34c-55f0-53bd-90d0-5654a556e400" :answer/id #uuid "ce19c34c-55f0-53bd-90d0-5654a556e400"] ["answer-ce19c34c-55f0-53bd-90d0-5654a556e400" :answer/type :answer.type/text] ["answer-ce19c34c-55f0-53bd-90d0-5654a556e400" :answer/value "I and II only"] ["answer-3df4871b-4533-56fb-bc7b-dd5a4905e0bc" :answer/id #uuid "3df4871b-4533-56fb-bc7b-dd5a4905e0bc"] ["answer-3df4871b-4533-56fb-bc7b-dd5a4905e0bc" :answer/type :answer.type/text] ["answer-3df4871b-4533-56fb-bc7b-dd5a4905e0bc" :answer/value "I and III only"] ["answer-e0274858-530a-5336-a244-3ab577098764" :answer/id #uuid "e0274858-530a-5336-a244-3ab577098764"] ["answer-e0274858-530a-5336-a244-3ab577098764" :answer/type :answer.type/text] ["answer-e0274858-530a-5336-a244-3ab577098764" :answer/value "I, II, and III"] ["answer-baae5ccd-bb5f-5df3-b5b6-228fe5505245" :answer/id #uuid "baae5ccd-bb5f-5df3-b5b6-228fe5505245"] ["answer-baae5ccd-bb5f-5df3-b5b6-228fe5505245" :answer/type :answer.type/text] ["answer-baae5ccd-bb5f-5df3-b5b6-228fe5505245" :answer/value "II and III only"] ["answer-d12f7ee4-e6d2-572c-bc6f-c2a4f216d210" :answer/id #uuid "d12f7ee4-e6d2-572c-bc6f-c2a4f216d210"] ["answer-d12f7ee4-e6d2-572c-bc6f-c2a4f216d210" :answer/type :answer.type/text] ["answer-d12f7ee4-e6d2-572c-bc6f-c2a4f216d210" :answer/value "II only"] ["answer-field-dd1c7fcd-33d6-50f3-a903-c3e604c9e00d" :answer-field/key "selection"] ["answer-field-dd1c7fcd-33d6-50f3-a903-c3e604c9e00d" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio] ["answer-field-dd1c7fcd-33d6-50f3-a903-c3e604c9e00d" :answer-field/correct "answer-e0274858-530a-5336-a244-3ab577098764"] ["answer-field-dd1c7fcd-33d6-50f3-a903-c3e604c9e00d" :answer-field/choices "answer-ce19c34c-55f0-53bd-90d0-5654a556e400"] ["answer-field-dd1c7fcd-33d6-50f3-a903-c3e604c9e00d" :answer-field/choices "answer-3df4871b-4533-56fb-bc7b-dd5a4905e0bc"] ["answer-field-dd1c7fcd-33d6-50f3-a903-c3e604c9e00d" :answer-field/choices "answer-e0274858-530a-5336-a244-3ab577098764"] ["answer-field-dd1c7fcd-33d6-50f3-a903-c3e604c9e00d" :answer-field/choices "answer-baae5ccd-bb5f-5df3-b5b6-228fe5505245"] ["answer-field-dd1c7fcd-33d6-50f3-a903-c3e604c9e00d" :answer-field/choices "answer-d12f7ee4-e6d2-572c-bc6f-c2a4f216d210"] ["question-f4fa3c45-5e91-57f0-a165-1adda22fdfd5" :question/answer-fields "answer-field-dd1c7fcd-33d6-50f3-a903-c3e604c9e00d"] [17592186054820 :knowledge-point/questions "question-f4fa3c45-5e91-57f0-a165-1adda22fdfd5"] [17592186056004 :tutorial/content "A **mathematical statement** is a sentence or mathematical expression that is either **true** or **false**.\n\nFor example:\n\n- The sentence, \"The sum of $1$ and $2$ is $3$\" is a mathematical statement because it is *true.*\n\n- Likewise, \"$2+2=5$\" is a mathematical statement because it is *false.*\n\nThe following examples are *not* mathematical statements:\n\n- \"Find the sum of $1$ and $2$\" is *not* a mathematical statement because it is neither true nor false. It is an instruction.\n\n- \"What is the sum of $1$ and $2?$\" is *not* a mathematical statement because it is neither true nor false. It is a question.\n\n- Similarly, \"$6+9$\" is *not* a mathematical statement because it is neither true nor false.\n\nWe often use upper-case letters such as $P, Q,$ and $R$ to represent statements. For example:\n\n- $P:$ The sum of two even integers is odd.\n\n- $Q: 3 \\in \\mathbb Z$\n\n- $R: \\{2,4,6\\ldots\\}\\cup \\{1,3,5,\\ldots\\} = \\mathbb N$\n\nIn this case, the statements $Q$ and $R$ are true, whereas statement $P$ is false."] [17592186056051 :tutorial/content "A **predicate** (or **open sentence**) of one variable is a sentence that contains a variable defined on some *universal set* and has the property that when we substitute a concrete value of the variable into the predicate, the result becomes a mathematical statement that is either *true* or *false*.\n\nFor example, consider the following predicate:\n\n$x > 1$\n\nThis is a predicate because substituting concrete values for $x$ gives a mathematical statement.\n\nFor instance: \n\n- Substituting $x=2$ gives the *true* statement \"$2 > 1$\"\n\n- Substituting $x=5$ gives the *true* statement \"$5 > 1$\"\n\n- Substituting $x=0$ gives the *false* statement \"$0 > 1$\"\n\nWe denote predicates using capital letters and the corresponding variable in parentheses. In this example, we can write our predicate more formally as follows:\n\n$P(x):$ $x > 1$\n\nAny predicate must be defined with its **universal set,** which is the set from which we are allowed to pull the concrete values of the variable. So, we could state our predicate more formally as\n\n$P(x):$ $x > 1,$ $\\quad$ where $x \\in \\mathbb{R}.$\n\nThe universal set can often be identified from the context and doesn't need to be explicitly stated. For example, if we have the predicate \n\n$n$ is divisible by 5,\n\nwe can assume that $n$ is defined on the set of all integers, i.e., $n \\in \\mathbb{Z}.$\n\nIn math, *any equation or inequality is a predicate*. However, a sentence containing a variable is not necessarily a predicate. For example, the sentence \n\n$\\qquad$ \"What is the sign of $y?$\"\n\nis *not* a predicate because substituting a concrete value for $y$ does not give a mathematical statement. Instead, it gives a question. \n\nSimilarly, \n\n$z^2-5$\n\nis *not* a predicate either. It's just an expression."] [17592186054913 :activity/expected-seconds 420.0]] "retractions" [[17592186054451 :question/problem "Which of the following are mathematical predicates of one variable?\n\n- Subtract $x$ from $10.$\n\n- $y^2 -2  \\leq 3$\n\n- $1+z$ is a perfect square."] [17592186056004 :tutorial/content "A **mathematical statement** is a sentence or mathematical expression that is either **true** or **false**.\n\nFor example:\n\n- The sentence, \"The sum of $1$ and $2$ is $3$\" is a mathematical statement because it is *true.*\n\n- Likewise, \"$2+2=5$\" is a mathematical statement because it is *false.*\n\nThe following examples are *not* mathematical statements:\n\n- \"Find the sum of $1$ and $2$\" is *not* a mathematical statement because it is neither true nor false. It is an instruction.\n\n- \"What is the sum of $1$ and $2?$\" is *not* a mathematical statement because it is neither true nor false. It is a question.\n\n- Similarly, \"$6+9$\" is *not* a mathematical statement because it is neither true nor false.\n\nWe often use upper-case letters such as $P, Q,$ and $R$ to represent statements. For example:\n\n- $P:$ The sum of two even integers is odd.\n\n- $Q: 3 \\in \\mathbb Z$\n\n- $R: \\{2,4,6\\ldots\\}\\cup \\{1,3,5,\\ldots\\} = \\mathbb N$\n\nIn this case, the statements $Q$ and $R$ are true, whereas statement $P$ is false.\n\nWe often refer to the **truth value** of a mathematical statement. We use [math]T[/math] to denote \"true\" and [math]F[/math] to denote \"false.\""] [17592186056051 :tutorial/content "A **predicate** (or **open sentence**) of one variable is a sentence that contains a variable defined on some *universal set* and has the property that when we substitute a concrete value of the variable into the predicate, the result becomes a mathematical statement that is either *true* or *false*.\n\nFor example, consider the following predicate:\n\n> $x > 1$\n\nThis is a predicate because substituting concrete values for $x$ gives a mathematical statement.\n\nFor instance: \n\n- Substituting $x=2$ gives the *true* statement \"$2 > 1$\"\n\n- Substituting $x=5$ gives the *true* statement \"$5 > 1$\"\n\n- Substituting $x=0$ gives the *false* statement \"$0 > 1$\"\n\nWe denote predicates using capital letters and the corresponding variable in parentheses. In this example, we can write our predicate more formally as follows:\n\n> $P(x):$ $x > 1$\n\nAny predicate must be defined with its **universal set,** which is the set from which we are allowed to pull the concrete values of the variable. So, we could state our predicate more formally as\n\n> $P(x):$ $x > 1,$ $\\quad$ where $x \\in \\mathbb{R}.$\n\nThe universal set can often be identified from the context and doesn't need to be explicitly stated. For example, if we have the predicate \n\n> $n$ is divisible by 5,\n\nwe can assume that $n$ is defined on the set of all integers, i.e., $n \\in \\mathbb{Z}.$\n\nIn math, *any equation or inequality is a predicate*. However, a sentence containing a variable is not necessarily a predicate. For example, the sentence \n\n$\\qquad$ \"What is the sign of $y?$\"\n\nis *not* a predicate because substituting a concrete value for $y$ does not give a mathematical statement. Instead, it gives a question. \n\nSimilarly, \n\n> $z^2-5$\n\nis *not* a predicate either. It's just an expression."]]}]